  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Sunday)\nIssued at 234 PM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nWhat a beautiful afternoon! Skies are just about clear across the\nentire region, with highs approaching 60 degrees in many areas! \nSome high-level clouds will drift across the region tonight, but \nthey shouldn't be impactful or last terribly long. Despite still \nnot seeing any significant change in synoptic wind direction or \noverall airmass, the low-levels should lean just a touch drier \ntonight into Monday morning compared to the past few days as\nreflected across all model guidance, and given a trend toward \nless daily fog and low stratus that was observed this morning (and\nperhaps some light interference to ideal radiational cooling \ncourtesy of those high clouds drifting through), we are generally \nnot expecting anything more than a few isolated, very patchy \ninstances of fog or mist. This has been left out of the forecast \nat this time due to very low confidence in occurrence/development\nand location, and limited expected impact.\n\nOtherwise, the forecast over the next few days continues to lean \nmild, dry, and slightly breezy for some areas as the central US \nremains mostly dominated by longwave ridging, with longwave \ntroughing residing along the Pacific coast. Several shortwave \npieces of energy attempt to break off/eject inland over the course\nof the next week. The first of any consequence...an organized \ntrough or even closed low tracking just to our north...comes \nthrough Wednesday with some potential for rain and snow showers, \nbut local QPF amounts continue to look quite light with this. In \nfact, much of the CWA may manage to remain predominantly dry. Our\ntemperatures will take a hit though, with highs in the 50s/60s \nTuesday only reaching the 40s/50s starting Wednesday. We are also\nnow starting to see signs that a jet streak will ride the base of\nthe trough/wrap around the southern nose of the closed low right \nover our general area Wednesday with potentially 50-60kts of flow \nin the 700-500mb layer (strongest on the GFS, but not far behind\non the Canadian and ECMWF). Some combination of a tightening \nhorizontal pressure gradient and/or mixing from aloft will result\nin breezy to windy conditions Wednesday afternoon and evening, \nespecially across our South Dakota plains, with southerly winds \nshifting to northwest with an associated cold front. NBM \nguidance (as we often see) is likely running too high advertising\npotential wind gusts of 55-65 MPH (granted despite a favorable \ndirection for stronger winds to be realized in our area). We also\nnote the deterministic NBM is running higher than the NBM 50th\npercentile at the moment. However, we are starting to see just a \ncouple GFS and ECMWF ensemble members gusting 45-55 MPH in point \nmeteograms for various towns and cities north, east, and south of\nthe Black Hills, a signal that wasn't there 24 hours ago perhaps \ndependent on eventual stability of the atmospheric column and how\nefficient mixing becomes, especially post-front. At the very \nleast, advisory-level winds are possible for some areas if the \ncurrent forecast holds. Still some time for things to trend here \nthough, including the strength and track of the shortwave/low and \nassociated jet streak.\n\nA much more significant inland-ejection of the coastal trough \nremains expected Friday into next Saturday with a better chance \nof more widespread precipitation (and a continued cooling of \ntemperatures...think increasing chance of snow). Confidence in QPF\ndetails remains low at this early juncture though...if anything, \nthe overall QPF and potential snowfall forecasts have steadily \ntrended down/decreased over the last several forecast updates. \nThis feature could weaken as it crosses the Rockies, with 500mb \nheight cluster analysis EOF patterns pointing at \nstrength/amplitude being the primary driver of forecast \nuncertainty at this time (compared to exact timing for our area, \nalthough that could eventually change too). Post-system drier but \nstill cool conditions may return for next Sunday. Highs only in\nthe upper 30s to lower 40s next weekend will run about 5 degrees \ncolder than climatology for late November.",
